{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1007306-ruins-of-the-time-before-thread","id":1007306,"title":"Ruins of the Time Before Thread","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","Chronicles of Darkness","Mage: The Awakening"],"posts":[{"date":"2016-11-23T10:18:21-08:00","id":1007306,"title":"Ruins of the Time Before Thread","content":"This is just a general thread for discussing Atlantis and its many shadows cast into the Fallen World. What are your best stories involving Atlantean Ruins? What are your favourite things about them? Got any ideas you'd love to use for traps? Story threads?<br /> <br /> I'm currently running a game involving a pyramid built around a device that can view alternate histories and, perhaps, make them real. The PCs are not the first to arrive on the scene, so most of the traps and wards are already disabled. Instead, it's inhabited by the mad time clones of the Mage who first discovered it, one of whom believes he can rewrite history so humanity never arose and are instead replaced by a saurian species capable of accessing the Supernal; destroying the Exarchs through abnegation of sympathy and letting a new species prosper instead. That's about all I can convey on a phone, though.<br /> <br /> What are the rest of you musing on?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2855-axelgear","name":"Axelgear","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2855_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1450"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-11-23T11:30:14-08:00","id":1007346,"title":"","content":"One concept that I was playing around with (in 1e) was a ruin that drained the mana out of the surrounding area. (The 2e Tokyo write-up gives rules to use for the draining effect.) Basically, my initial notion was that there was some research on the Abyss going on Pre Fall, and they set up some powerful warding to lock it out of reality. Post Fall, well, the wards are overwhelmed and <i>hungry</i>, which causes them to consume mana like mad. The guardians in the thing are powerful as all heck, but rely on an availability of mana that is simply untenable in the modern age, even to a master of Prime.<br /> <br /> The Bound that the facility was investigating was not freed, but was able to leak out a little.<br /> <br /> The idea I had for the story was that the stories about a left-handed legacy cropping up would be the first clue. The legacy would claim to be the heroes of the tale and that they are now guardians of reality who need vast amounts of mana to perform their miracles. The counter argument is that the legacy is abyssal, and both might even be true; if some were tainted and those attainments are almost indistinguishable, that could make the distinction really dangerous and difficult to make.<br /> <br /> Exploring a magical ruin when magic becomes unpredictable and flaky would make things far more tense.<br /> Interesting twists could be had if there is a seer presence maintaining the seals. &quot;Oh, hey! We don't have to sacrifice friends this time! Thanks for coming.&quot;<br /> <br /> Principle of emphasis was that the Supernal was closer in the Time Before, so the things that were serious back then took mana that's absurd now. Unlocking even just a considerable fraction of that proper capacity essentially turns the mage into a being that hungers for more of the Supernal just to survive. Thus, the mana appetites of the guardians and those who learn to shape their souls like them. This take could mean that many surviving ruins would have such guardians lurking within, which would also mean that multiple ruins might originate variations on the legacy and have areas with draining mana.<br /> <br /> Another thought was that there could be a 1st age warrior or even just guardian creation designed to wake and crush some specific monster, but the lack of mana precludes it from fully rousing. (That allows for some ley line adjustments and/or multiple temple romps to get the thing running properly again, but only at some sacrifice.)","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Grump, grouse, and/or gripe.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/672-thenate","name":"thenate","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar672_1.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"928"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-11-24T18:34:22-08:00","id":1007896,"title":"","content":"I personally really like the Mystery of the Hearth in the Mysterium book. The chance to chase mana through the astral realms where it will bring you back to Atlantis (should you succeed in the ritual) is pretty darn amazing. The whole idea that Atlantis still exists and the Mysterium is keeping it alive is such a big middle finger to the exarchs that it makes me chuckle with glee.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/7825-falcon777","name":"Falcon777","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Sep 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"490"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}